The '''1992 United States Senate election in New Hampshire''' took place on November 3, 1992. Incumbent Republican Senator [[Warren Rudman]] declined to seek re-election. Governor [[Judd Gregg]] won the Republican primary to succeed him, and faced businessman John Rauh, the Democratic nominee, in the general election. Gregg narrowly defeated Rauh, winning 48 percent of the vote to Rauh's 45 percent, his narrowest victory in any statewide race.

==Democratic primary==
===Candidates===
* John Rauh, [[Sunapee, New Hampshire|Sunapee]] businessman, [[1990 United States Senate election in New Hampshire|1990]] Democratic candidate for the U.S. Senate<ref name="seek recognition">{{cite news|last=Chiacu|first=Doina|title=Democrats seek recognition: Governor better known in Senate race|newspaper=[[Concord Monitor]]|date=July 6, 1992|location=[[Concord, New Hampshire]]|page=B2|url=https://www.newspapers.com/article/concord-monitor/174670042/|access-date=June 16, 2025}}</ref>
* Brenda J. Elias, Mayor of [[Franklin, New Hampshire|Franklin]]<ref name="seek recognition"/>
* Terry Bennett, [[Rochester, New Hampshire|Rochester]] physician<ref name="seek recognition"/>
* Jeanne Stapleton, [[John Stark Regional High School]] teacher<ref name="women candidates">{{cite news|last=Chiacu|first=Doina|title=Women Candidates Hope To Make Changes|newspaper=[[Valley News]]|date=July 6, 1992|location=[[Lebanon, New Hampshire]]|page=12|url=https://www.newspapers.com/article/valley-news/174669994/|access-date=June 16, 2025}}</ref>
* Lynn Rudmin Chong, former instructor at [[Plymouth State College]]<ref name="women candidates"/>

